\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$. Specialized notation and index ranges follow the cited source and the definitions below.


A minimax estimator minimizes the largest risk over a stated model class. For a predictor $f$, distributional risk is $R_P(f)=\mathbb E_{z\sim P}[\ell(f,z)]$ for the specified loss. A distribution shift replaces $P$ by $Q$.
\paragraph{Record scope and qualifications.}
The catalogue title is retained as metadata. Named certificates, contracts, bounds or frameworks in that title are not asserted as results of the original source extract.
The signal/noise models, loss, admissible estimators, regularizers, and shift class are not supplied; the three directions are kept distinct.

Source-record limitation: The signal and noise families and the quantifiers for the proposed risk monotonicity are unspecified; no definite minimax value or equivalence criterion can be recovered.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Characterize minimax-optimal estimators for broad signal and worst-case noise models. When are robust-optimization formulations equivalent to regularized estimation, and when is in-distribution risk $R_P(f)$ monotonically related to shifted-distribution risk $R_Q(f)$? \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2022/46}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this source record open; that metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. The label is preserved for provenance and is not an independent verification of present open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-22.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Characterize optimal worst-case estimators and relationships between robust, regularized, and shifted-data risk.

